From 11766a8c2e21cdb2e2ddec170be4c43496393187 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:33:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] GBM: HDR10 and HDR10+ adapted to the display by the player
 (videoscreen.hadeshdr10adapt)

"Map to the display" (videoscreen.hadesdvhdr10map) existed only for
Dolby Vision rebuilt as HDR10. A projector, or a display that maps less
well than the player does, gets HDR10 and HDR10+ as mastered and maps
them itself. avdvplus has an automatic choice that adapts only where the
master is brighter than the display (its notes, read as data).

The bridge already maps an HDR10 picture that is not reconstructed
(dvbridge_render_plain(): the BT.2390 curve guided by the static HDR10
metadata of the stream, the perceptual gamut mapping), to SDR where the
output takes no HDR; its mapping can be to an HDR10 display as well. So
the picture goes that way, as it is: it is not promoted to Dolby Vision
(the measurement of "HDR10 as Dolby Vision" exists only where native
output draws, and a promoted stream would go out natively), and no RPU
is composed. Where the output takes HDR10 and HDR10_ADAPT::Decide() says
so (decided once per video, with its first picture asked), the renderer
is set up as for the mapping to SDR, with the output told HDR10 at the
peak of the display (where the curve maps from above it: the mastering
peak and MaxCLL of the display, MaxCLL kept where it is lower, MaxFALL
not known; the primaries of the gamut mapped into; never HDR10+, whose
dynamic metadata describe the picture as mastered).

The peak of the display is the one set for the mapping of Dolby Vision
to the display where that is on (with its gamut and its curve), else the
desired content max luminance of its EDID (CDisplayInfo::
DesiredContentMaxLuminance()); BT.2020 and BT.2390 where nothing is set.

System > Display: "HDR10 and HDR10+ adapted to the display": Never (the
default: the display maps the video as it was mastered), Always,
Automatic. English and Spanish.

Fail closed: a renderer that cannot draw it (not VAAPI), a mapping that
cannot be made, an output that does not take HDR10 for it, or 48
pictures in a row that cannot be mapped: the video goes out as HDR10 as
it is, the display maps it, as before.

kodi.log: the decision and why (also why not: the setting automatic and
a video not brighter, no peak given, and so on), the mapping, what the
output is told, and every 10 s what it costs: "HDR10 adapted to the
display, last 10 s: N pictures mapped, ...; GPU time A ms on average, M
at most, of K timed (refresh period P ms)", with GL timestamps read back
on a later frame, never waited for, as for native output; and at the
end. The player information gives a row "HDR10 and HDR10+ adapted to
the display": yes (brighter, or always) with the two peaks, no (not
brighter), or no (kodi.log says why).

With the setting off nothing changes: the renderer is asked one more
question per picture, whose answer is no.
